/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 516 0 R
/T (6.1.3 Bio- Plugs These are small bio-plugs to be constructed by vegetation leaves and dead wood logs or pine needles and soil acrossan ephemeral rill/gully.The purpose of these bio-plugs is to break the speed of runoff in the ephemeral rill/gully andto store rainwater to facilitate it for groundwater recharge \(Plate-X\). The size of these bio-plugs depends upon the local terrain condition.In the study area, there are two rills/gullies. About 20 bio-plugs at a vertical interval shall be needed across in the upper reaches of both the rills/gullies \(Fig.13\). This work should be done in association with  department of Forest and Watershed Management and Soil Conservation.  6.1.4 Rill Plugs/Gully Plugs Due to high intensity rain events, the hill slopes are being eroded steadily through the runoff along ephemeral channels by which deep rills and gulliesare being developed. To control this process of riling/gullying, there is need to construct plugs of stones across these rills/gullies to decrease the velocity of screams, to store rainwater for infiltration and to trap the sediment. The size of such plugs \(Plate-XI\) depends upon the local terrain condition. In the study area 5 gully plugs shall be needed in the middle reaches across 3 ephemeral channels \(Fig.13\).This work should be done in  association with department of Watershed Management and Soil Conservation. )
/E (6.1.3 Bio- Plugs These are small bio-plugs to be constructed by vegetation leaves and dead wood logs or pine needles and soil acrossan ephemeral rill/gully.The purpose of these bio-plugs is to break the speed of runoff in the ephemeral rill/gully andto store rainwater to facilitate it for groundwater recharge \(Plate-X\). The size of these bio-plugs depends upon the local terrain condition.In the study area, there are two rills/gullies. About 20 bio-plugs at a vertical interval shall be needed across in the upper reaches of both the rills/gullies \(Fig.13\). This work should be done in association with  department of Forest and Watershed Management and Soil Conservation.  6.1.4 Rill Plugs/Gully Plugs Due to high intensity rain events, the hill slopes are being eroded steadily through the runoff along ephemeral channels by which deep rills and gulliesare being developed. To control this process of riling/gullying, there is need to construct plugs of stones across these rills/gullies to decrease the velocity of screams, to store rainwater for infiltration and to trap the sediment. The size of such plugs \(Plate-XI\) depends upon the local terrain condition. In the study area 5 gully plugs shall be needed in the middle reaches across 3 ephemeral channels \(Fig.13\).This work should be done in  association with department of Watershed Management and Soil Conservation. )
/Pg 38 0 R
/K [2 3 4]
>>
